on likely did something similar but maybe not optimally? Let's check the logs of a69e622158bd4a3d: it was correct and faster. We don't have its source code, only that it used \"integral image (summed-area table)\". So we can try to implement an even more optimized version with:\n\n- Use 1D array for matrix and prefix sum.\n- Loop interchange for better cache usage when computing prefix sums: compute row-wise then column-wise? Actually typical integral image computation is two passes: first horizontal cumulative sum, then vertical. That's already O(N^2) and memory-friendly if done in order.\n\nBut maybe we can also apply blocking to the submatrix search loop? Since after building prefix sum, finding max sum involves iterating over all top-left corners (i from 0 to N-S, j from 0 to N-S). This is a double loop with O(N^2) iterations. We could block this loop for better cache reuse of the prefix sum array.\n\nAlternatively, we can try to use SIMD or unrolling? But that's not memory locality per se.\n\nGiven our role, I'll propose:\n\nHypothesis: \"Using a flat 1D layout for both matrix and integral image improves spatial locality. Computing the integral image with two passes (row-wise then column-wise) ensures sequential access. The submatrix sum evaluation loop is blocked to improve temporal reuse of prefix sums.\"\n\nTransformations:\n- Flatten matrix into 1D array.\n- Compute row-wise prefix sums in-place, then compute final integral image by accumulating columns.\n- Use blocking for the max-sum search: iterate over blocks of top-left corners to keep relevant parts of prefix sum in cache.\n\nBut we must ensure correctness. The previous attempt with flattening (63c31fec64a84411) was incorrect due to \"Reference output mismatch\". That might have been a bug, not necessarily because of flattening. So we need to be careful.\n\nAlternatively, we could stick with the sliding window but improve memory locality by using 1D array and loop interchange? The baseline already use