um with blocking\n    const int B = 256; // block size, tune later\n    int mxSum = -2147483648;\n    for(int ii=0; ii<=n-s; ii+=B) {\n        int i_end = std::min(ii+B, n-s+1);\n        for(int jj=0; jj<=n-s; jj+=B) {\n            int j_end = std::min(jj+B, n-s+1);\n            for(int i=ii; i<i_end; ++i) {\n                const int base_i_s = (i+s)*np1;\n                const int base_i = i*np1;\n                for(int j=jj; j<j_end; ++j) {\n                    int sum = pref[base_i_s + (j+s)] - pref[base_i + (j+s)]\n                              - pref[base_i_s + j] + pref[base_i + j];\n                    if(sum > mxSum) mxSum = sum;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", mxSum);\n}\n\nBut note: The loops for ii and jj should go up to n-s inclusive. So condition ii <= n-s, but we need i_end = min(ii+B, n-s+1). Actually if ii goes in steps of B, the last block may exceed n-s. We'll set limit as while (ii < n-s+1) then process block.\n\nBetter: for(int ii=0; ii<n-s+1; ii+=B) { int i_end = std::min(ii+B, n-s+1); ... }\n\nSimilarly for jj.\n\nAlso we need to include <algorithm> for min and max? We'll use std::max but we already have it from algorithm. Or define our own.\n\nWe also need to ensure that s is less than n, etc., as per assertions.\n\nNow check correctness: The integral image formula uses pref[i][j] = sum of submatrix (0..i-1, 0..j-1). So for top-left corner (i,j) and size s, the sum is pref[i+s][j+s] - pref[i][j+s] - pref[i+s][j] + pref[i][j]. That's correct.\n\nWe must be careful with indices: we used base_i = i*np1 where np1=n+1. So pref[base_i + j] corresponds to row i, column j (0-indexed). Since our array is of size (n+1)*(n+1), and we set first row/col to zero.\n\nIn horizontal pass: for each i from 0..n-1, we compute cumulative sum along columns into pref[(i+1)*np1 + (j+1)]. That's correct because after vertical pass it becomes integral image.\n\nVertical pass adds the value from previous row at same column.